{
  "dom_id": "step-q317883",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-3}?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/2a5b83a177193d9a6be738cd521410fa3369afaec3f88f121d42616c51313696.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $3$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/905c163e5836693180cd5ccee9af63f875433c243ef630126762df3fe74f98d5.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-3}.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/03b657beccb9c7afd2c652f44ba70cf15c186463715acafe35df10e650831aec.png)",
